--- MESSAGE ---
Mutable collections are [T]+, {T}+ and {K, V}+, and the Java lists leave the definition

A trailing `+` on a collection bracket names its mutable version wherever a type is written, carried as
the node key; `fix` and `mut` speak only of the name, because a container's mutability must be in its
type to cross a call. The Scala target spells a mutable buffer, set or map, an empty literal in a `+`
slot as the mutable empty, and the one operation the `+` grants, `add(xs, x)`, as an in-place add. The
four collectors an actor's caller hands in and a rule appends to are `[T]+`, Accumulator's `mut {T}`
host text becomes `{Int}+` and the last host-text type form retires with it. `()+` is reserved for
mutable trees. No host head is left in the corpus; the leaves remain.

